# CITATION.cff -- how to cite the index (P0-S7-T02; 05-repository-and-workflow.md S10).
#
# GitHub renders this as "Cite this repository". `doi` is the Zenodo CONCEPT DOI (P0-S7-T05): it
# is version-independent and resolves to the latest version, so it is the one to cite. Each version
# has its own DOI as well (v0.1.0 is 10.5281/zenodo.23045828). Versions are added to the Zenodo
# record by hand, not by the Zenodo-GitHub integration, which would mint a second concept DOI for
# the same work: docs/releasing.md has the steps.
# Contributors who curate entries are credited here as well as on the entries (05 S7; 00 S12).

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use the Universal AI Benchmark Index, please cite it as below. The data is a git
  repository of YAML files: cite the release, or the commit hash you used, so that the version
  you read can be recovered.
type: dataset
title: The Universal AI Benchmark Index
abstract: >-
  A faceted, source-linked, version-controlled catalogue of AI benchmarks across every domain.
  Every number traces to a primary source and carries the conditions that produced it as
  structured data, and the catalogue refuses to rank numbers whose conditions differ. It never
  hosts benchmark data, never runs evaluations and never computes a universal score.
authors:
  - name: Tatra Labs
repository-code: https://github.com/benchmarkg/benchmarks
# The data is CC-BY-4.0 and the code MIT (LICENSE-DATA, LICENSE-CODE; REUSE.toml maps every path).
license:
  - CC-BY-4.0
  - MIT
keywords:
  - AI benchmarks
  - evaluation
  - benchmark catalogue
  - provenance
  - taxonomy
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23045827
